%% @fig 01
%% @kicker Inference optimization stack
%% @alt The inference stack, top to bottom: serving engines (vLLM, TensorRT-LLM, SGLang), continuous batching, the Flash Attention 2 kernel, PagedAttention for the KV cache, quantization from FP8 to INT8 to 4-bit, and the GPU. Numbered tags give the order to apply them: continuous batching first, Flash Attention 2 second, FP8 third, PagedAttention fourth, INT8 or 4-bit last.
%% @caption Apply the levers in the numbered order. The basics move throughput more than the sophisticated techniques do.
%% @dim batch | quant | Four software levers
%% @table Order of application | # | Technique | Typical effect | Trade-off
%% @row 1 | Continuous batching | 10–20× | Memory reserved for max batch
%% @row 2 | Flash Attention 2 | 2–4× | Baseline; O(n) memory
%% @row 3 | FP8 quantization | 40–50% | Modest speedup
%% @row 4 | PagedAttention | 2–4× | Long contexts, high concurrency
%% @row 5 | INT8 or 4-bit | 1–3% | Quality loss; 5–8% on code
%% @detail kv | KV cache · 70B, 128K context | One sequence: ≈ 64 GB; 16 sequences: ≈ 1 TB
%% @detail batch | Over-reserved batch | max_num_seqs: 256; Live sequences: ≈ 30; Phantom slots: 226
